"Explains a procedure through ordered actions, explicit decisions and clearly marked entry and exit points.\n\nUse when: Teach a bounded operational procedure. Locate repeated work, missing decisions or ambiguous outcomes.\n\nAvoid when: Formal event semantics, participant messaging or detailed concurrency must be executable.\n\nVisual grammar: Action boxes contain short verb phrases. Decision diamonds contain questions with labeled outgoing alternatives. Directional connectors express the next step; terminators mark starts and ends.\n\nBuild: State the trigger and intended completion condition. Record the observed steps before introducing decision branches. Trace normal, rejected and exceptional cases with process participants.\n\nCheck: Every decision has complete, distinguishable alternatives. Every action is reachable from an entry point. Loops have an explicit return condition and possible exit.\n\nPitfalls: Using diamonds for actions rather than questions. Connecting boxes without indicating the direction of progression.\n\nExample: A library renewal procedure checks overdue status, verifies eligibility, renews the loan or explains the refusal to the borrower.\n\nAlternatives: Swimlane flowchart, BPMN process."
